WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
On the web, adding a new feature is a frictionless process.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.500
Type NPM install and watch the code seamlessly merge into your digital pipeline.

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.500
Web developers have built an entire operational habit around this.

4
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.000
You run a package manager, let the bundler handle tree shaking to drop dead code,

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.500
and move on to building the UI.

6
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.500
But when you transition to React Native,

7
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.500
the assumption that dependencies are operationally cheap breaks down immediately.

8
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.500
A mobile dependency doesn't just sit in a single browser environment.

9
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:36.000
It has to navigate two entirely different execution models simultaneously,

10
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.000
communicating between your JavaScript logic and the underlying mobile operating system.

11
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:45.000
If you treat a React Native dependency tree exactly like a web apps,

12
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:49.000
you guarantee pipeline friction and inevitable build failures.

13
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.500
To avoid that friction, you have to recognize that React Native packages

14
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.500
are strictly bifurcated into two distinct risk profiles.

15
00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:00.000
The first profile consists of JavaScript-only packages.

16
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.000
These are your standard utility libraries, like low-dash or state management tools,

17
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.000
like Zestand and Redux.

18
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.000
These carry a very low-risk profile.

19
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.500
Because they contain only JavaScript code,

20
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.500
they are resolved entirely by the Metro Bundler.

21
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:20.500
They behave exactly as they do in the DOM, requiring no special compilation.

22
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:24.500
The second profile involves packages containing native code.

23
00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:29.500
These interact directly with device hardware, like the camera, GPS mapping modules,

24
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:31.500
or gyroscope sensors.

25
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.500
Look inside these packages in your Node modules folder,

26
00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:41.000
and you'll find nested Android and iOS sub-directories containing raw Kotlin, Java, or Swift files.

27
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.500
These bypass standard JavaScript tools.

28
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.100
They cannot be bundled.

29
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:49.500
They must be compiled by native toolchains every single time you build.

30
00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:52.500
Identifying which category a package falls into

31
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:57.000
allows you to map its impact on the build process before the first line of code is written.

32
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.000
That architectural cost manifests in four distinct ways.

33
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.500
The first is region version bubbling.

34
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:07.000
A native package is highly sensitive to the environment around it.

35
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:12.000
It must perfectly align with the specific version of the react native core you are currently running.

36
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.000
This matrix shows a central native dependency linked to four pillars.

37
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:21.000
React native core, Android Gradle, Xcode, and your Expo SDK.

38
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.500
This creates a cascading failure state.

39
00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:28.000
If you upgrade the core framework, that dependency must have a compatible update ready,

40
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.000
otherwise compilation fails.

41
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Adopting a native package means your project's upgrade timeline is now hostage

42
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:37.000
to that specific library's open source maintainers.

43
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.500
The second cost involves build times and the debugging domain.

44
00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.000
On the web, if you introduce a problematic package,

45
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:46.500
the errors are cleanly surfaced in your Bundler's terminal output.

46
00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:50.000
You fix the syntax, save the file, and the browser reloads.

47
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.500
Native dependency errors bypass the JavaScript Bundler entirely.

48
00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:59.500
Metro will report a successful build even while the app itself fails to compile and launch.

49
00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.000
When a build fails due to a native package,

50
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:08.000
syntax or version errors trigger massive complex stack traces deep within Gradle or Xcode.

51
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:13.000
Debugging these failures is an entirely different operational skill than debugging JavaScript.

52
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:18.000
It requires a baseline fluency and reading platform specific tool chain logs.

53
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.500
The third cost center centers on architectural deprecation and compatibility.

54
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.000
React Native is undergoing a massive structural transition,

55
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.500
moving away from its original asynchronous communication bridge between JavaScript and native threads.

56
00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:37.500
The framework is adopting a new synchronous execution model based on C++ interfaces.

57
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:41.000
This new architecture is the required standard moving forward.

58
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:46.000
However, thousands of community native packages still rely on the legacy bridge.

59
00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:52.000
If you integrate one of these packages, your app is forced to route data through the old slower pipeline,

60
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.000
bottlenecking performance.

61
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:02.000
By installing a legacy native dependency, you bind your modern application to the performance limitations of the old communication bridge.

62
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.000
The fourth and final hidden cost is deployment rigidity.

63
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:13.000
In a standard React Native lifecycle, over-the-air updates allow you to quickly swap out the JavaScript bundle to hot-fix bugs instantly,

64
00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:15.500
bypassing the App Store review process.

65
00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:18.000
This flow chart contrasts update paths.

66
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:23.000
On top, a lightweight JavaScript payload bypasses stores directly to the device.

67
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.000
On the bottom, native code changes require large binary blocks.

68
00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:33.000
Because native dependencies alter the compiled binary, they're immune to over-the-air deployment,

69
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:35.500
bottlenecking at App Store review.

70
00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:40.000
If you realize a native library is broken or causing crashes in production,

71
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.000
you have to execute a full native rebuild,

72
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:48.000
update your provisioning profiles, and submit a brand-new release to Apple and Google.

73
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:51.000
Once a native dependency is published in a live app,

74
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:56.000
the engineering team permanently loses the ability to hot-fix that specific feature.

75
00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:02.000
These four costs apply to Greenfield projects, built entirely in React Native from day one.

76
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.000
But they escalate rapidly in a brownfield environment,

77
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:10.000
where React Native is embedded inside a large, pre-existing native application.

78
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:16.000
In this context, the operational risks of introducing a native dependency are multiplied significantly.

79
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.000
A React Native package brings its own transitive dependencies,

80
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:23.000
native networking clients, or image processing tools,

81
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:28.000
that often go unseen until they conflict with the host app's existing environment.

82
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:34.000
This diagram shows a large host application container holding an embedded React Native container.

83
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.000
Watch what happens when the React Native package

84
00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:41.000
injects a library version the host app already uses.

85
00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:46.000
They collide, resulting in duplicate symbols and fatal compilation crashes.

86
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:51.000
In a brownfield environment, adding a native dependency is a highly invasive action.

87
00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:55.000
It requires direct architectural coordination with the host native team

88
00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:59.000
to resolve version conflicts before any code can run.

89
00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:03.000
To manage these risks, you must implement a strict pre-flight operational checklist

90
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.000
before ever running an installation command on a mobile project.

91
00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.000
The first check is simple.

92
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:15.000
Investigate the package repository, specifically for the presence of native Android or iOS directories.

93
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:18.000
A stay exists, the risk profile elevates.

94
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.000
The second check verifies compatibility with the new architecture.

95
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:28.000
Cross-reference the library on community directory databases to ensure it supports the synchronous bridge model.

96
00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:31.000
Finally, audit the packages maintenance status.

97
00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:35.000
Check its open issue queue and rigorously search for a JavaScript only alternative

98
00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:39.000
that could fulfill the exact same use case without touching the native layer.

99
00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:43.000
Every native dependency links into your app's compilation process,

100
00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:48.000
creating a long-term maintenance commitment that requires the same scrutiny as your core infrastructure.

